170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 512 0 R
/E (Nominal data is categorical data where the values represent categories with no inherent order or ranking. For example, eye color \(blue, brown, green\) or types of fruits \(apple, banana, orange\).)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
171 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 513 0 R
/E (Ordinal data, on the other hand, is categorical data with values that have a meaningful order or ranking. However, the intervals between the values may not be consistent. For example, rating scales like "poor," "fair," "good," and "excellent" or educational levels like "high school," "associate's degree," "bachelor's degree," and "master's degree.")
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
